  <section id="testing">
    <h2 class="logo-heading"><span class="name-light">Usability</span> <span class="name-bold">study, round 1</span></h2>
    <p>The low-fidelity prototype connected the main flow, building a pet profile and checking out with your recommended food. In December 2021 I ran an unmoderated, remote usability study with <strong>5 participants</strong> (3 men, 1 woman and 1 nonbinary person, ages 20 to 60), about 10 minutes each, to see whether that flow was easy to complete.</p>
    <div class="case-pains case-themes">
      <div>
        <p class="case-num">4 of 5</p>
        <h3>Didn’t see how to start a pet profile</h3>
        <p>They made two or more misclicks trying to get started.</p>
        <blockquote class="case-quote"><p>“The get started button didn’t look like a clickable thing.”</p></blockquote>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="case-num">3 of 5</p>
        <h3>Couldn’t find their recommendation</h3>
        <p>They had trouble spotting the “Check it out” button on the home page.</p>
        <blockquote class="case-quote"><p>“Was looking to see what the best food would be, and didn’t realize it was the selection at the top.”</p></blockquote>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="case-num">2 of 5</p>
        <h3>Found typing pet info frustrating</h3>
        <p>They worried free-text fields would give inaccurate results.</p>
        <blockquote class="case-quote"><p>“Make drop downs for more accurate results.”</p></blockquote>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p><strong>What I changed:</strong> the buttons got a more noticeable color and a pressed state so they look clickable; the small “Check it out” link became a large “Go to Recommended Pet Food” button, with the recommendation opening in an overlay that’s hard to miss; and most profile fields became drop-down menus instead of typing. You can see these in the wireframe and prototype comparisons above.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="testing-2">
    <h2 class="logo-heading"><span class="name-light">Usability</span> <span class="name-bold">study, round 2</span></h2>
    <p>I tested the high-fidelity prototype with six people in an unmoderated study in Maze, using two tasks:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Create a pet profile</strong> for Lassie, a female mixed border collie born January 4, 2020, weighing 80 pounds.</li>
      <li><strong>Order the matching food:</strong> add the recommended food to the cart, check out and complete the order.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="case-stats">
      <div class="case-stat-group">
        <h3>Task 1: Create a pet profile</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><span class="case-num">67%</span> completed it (4 of 6), but none took the expected path</li>
          <li><span class="case-num">2 of 6</span> gave up partway</li>
          <li><span class="case-num">64%</span> of clicks missed</li>
          <li><span class="case-num">2.5 min</span> on average</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="case-stat-group">
        <h3>Task 2: Order the matching food</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><span class="case-num">100%</span> completed it (4 of 4), half by the expected path</li>
          <li><span class="case-num">38%</span> of clicks missed</li>
          <li><span class="case-num">45 sec</span> on average</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>Ordering worked well. Building the profile was still where people struggled, and two needs stood out: <strong>more food recommendation options</strong>, and <strong>a clear way to enter mixed breed information</strong>.</p>
    <div class="case-findings">
      <div class="case-finding">
        {% picture "projects/petchecker/breed-list.png", "Pet's Breed list with a search bar, an Is your pet a mixed breed? checkbox and an alphabetical list of breeds with an A to Z index.", "(min-width: 48rem) 12rem, 40vw" %}
        <div>
          <h3>“Mixed breed” was confusing</h3>
          <p>The option showed up in two places. One participant asked why there were two checkboxes, and another tapped it and nothing happened.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="case-finding">
        {% picture "projects/petchecker/recommended-food.png", "Recommended Pet food pop-up showing Fresh Kisses Dog Food at $10.99, placeholder reasons it matches the profile, and an Add to Cart button.", "(min-width: 48rem) 12rem, 40vw" %}
        <div>
          <h3>One recommendation felt suspicious</h3>
          <p>A single food with no alternatives made one participant wonder whether the app was just selling one brand. Another didn’t expect Add to Cart to take them away from the page they were on.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="case-finding">
        {% picture "projects/petchecker/date-picker.png", "Birthdate picker labelled Month/Date/Year showing a grid of the twelve months.", "(min-width: 48rem) 12rem, 40vw" %}
        <div>
          <h3>The profile form felt rigid</h3>
          <p>Participants expected to pick the birth year first or type the date, wanted the pet’s name to come first, and found that the photo step blocked them from moving on.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="refining">
    <h2 class="logo-heading"><span class="name-light">Refining</span> <span class="name-bold">the design</span></h2>
    <p>I took the round 2 feedback back into Figma. To make choosing food easier without overwhelming anyone, I added two more recommendations below the best match, with just enough information to compare them.</p>
    <div class="case-findings">
      <div class="case-finding">
        {% picture "projects/petchecker/v2-recommendations.png", "Best Recommended Pet food pop-up with Fresh Kisses Dog Food and an Add to Cart button, followed by Other Great Pet Food Recommendations: two more foods, each with a Preview button.", "(min-width: 48rem) 12rem, 40vw" %}
        <div>
          <h3>More than one food to choose from</h3>
          <p>The best match now comes with other recommendations you can preview, so the app feels like it’s helping you choose rather than selling one brand.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="case-finding">
        {% picture "projects/petchecker/v2-mixed-breed.png", "Pop-up asking Is your pet a mixed breed? with Yes and No buttons.", "(min-width: 48rem) 12rem, 40vw" %}
        <div>
          <h3>Mixed breed is asked once</h3>
          <p>Instead of two checkboxes in two places, a single yes-or-no question.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="case-finding">
        {% picture "projects/petchecker/v2-added-to-cart.png", "Added Item To Cart! confirmation pop-up with the dog food image and buttons for Go to Cart and Go to Homepage.", "(min-width: 48rem) 12rem, 40vw" %}
        <div>
          <h3>Add to Cart keeps you in place</h3>
          <p>A confirmation pop-up replaces the jump to the cart, and you decide whether to check out now or keep browsing.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="case-finding">
        {% picture "projects/petchecker/v2-photo-step.png", "Select a Profile Picture screen showing a grid of photos of a black dog and a Next button.", "(min-width: 48rem) 12rem, 40vw" %}
        <div>
          <h3>A clearer photo step</h3>
          <p>Choosing a profile picture became its own screen with a photo grid, instead of a pop-up that blocked the form.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="case-links"><a href="https://www.figma.com/file/EBCEwGrZMWSmxmEdn9x8Om/Google-Project-%231-Wireframe?node-id=510%3A17669">View the high-fidelity prototype in Figma</a></p>
  </section>

  <section id="design-system">
    <h2 class="logo-heading"><span class="name-light">Design</span> <span class="name-bold">system and accessibility</span></h2>
    <p>A sticker sheet kept the screens consistent: Fira Sans for headings and Nanum Gothic for body text, a navy, gold, blue and copper palette, and shared buttons, navigation bars, a search bar, icons and calendar states.</p>
    <figure class="case-wide">{% picture "projects/petchecker/sticker-sheet.png", "PetChecker sticker sheet: the PetChecker logo; typography specs for Fira Sans headers and Nanum Gothic body and buttons; copper, blue and edit buttons, a quantity stepper and a dropdown; the navy footer navigation bar and top navigation bar; the search bar; icons for map, health, home, settings, help and log out; calendar states; and four color swatches, 2A3A83, BFAE5A, 658CBF and BF814B.", "(min-width: 72rem) 72rem, 100vw", "lazy", [800, 1440] %}</figure>
    <ul>
      <li>Icons have names below them to make navigation easier.</li>
      <li>High-contrast buttons make the user flow clearer.</li>
      <li>I used Material’s color tool to choose the color scheme so the contrast meets WCAG standards.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="takeaways">
    <h2 class="logo-heading"><span class="name-light">Key</span> <span class="name-bold">takeaways</span></h2>
    <p><strong>Impact:</strong> In round 2, everyone who reached checkout completed their order. And after the final redesign, the pet profile, the hardest part of testing, earned this from peer feedback:</p>
    <blockquote class="case-quote"><p>“It was very easy to create a pet profile. The steps were well broken out and well organized. Having an idea on how much progress you’ve made by the little progress bar was helpful as well.”</p></blockquote>
    <p><strong>What I learned:</strong> While designing PetChecker, I learned that designing the app is a tiny piece of the UX process. Usability testing showed me problems with the app that I had never even considered.</p>
    <p><strong>Next steps:</strong> A third round of testing on the redesign, measuring:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Whether all participants can create a pet profile (round 2: 4 of 6)</li>
      <li>Whether misclicks drop from 64%</li>
      <li>Whether people pick from the new recommendation options</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
